{% endhighlight %}

What we do is pretty simple:

1. We assume that there are true preconditions for all of the methods we are testing.
2. We create a new ArrayList instance.
3. For each of our methods we call the actual method with our parameters for the method under test.
4. We update the state of our tests following the method call.
5. We observe all state variables that we would like to display, this does not affect the actual assertion of our method.
6. Finally, we assert that our conditions are met based on our observations.

This does not mean that all the assertions are correct as it only demonstrates that there are certain conditions that could arise during the method execution, however it gives us a way to determine how the conditions impact the rest of the method under test.

The last assertion I mention is a bit more complex to demonstrate a more complex condition. Instead of a simple check for false, we check if the return value is false and check if the last index of x is lower than or equal to the index of x.

Of course, the last condition will still be incorrect, you need to check at least one instance where conditions you mentioned can be met and provide a counterexample.

This way you can get enough information about what the conditions of your methods were and what impact they had on your tests. This can then be used to refine your conditions and write more accurate assertions.
{% endhighlight %}

